    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism began in 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and his followers, who sought to revive the language, literature and moral wisdom of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</li><li>Humanists hunted for lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, learned Greek from Byzantine teachers and developed the textual criticism with which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a> as a forgery.</li><li>Carried across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and by scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, its course of grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history and moral philosophy shaped European schooling for four centuries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The roman type of most printed books today descends from a handwriting that Poggio Bracciolini and his Florentine circle developed around 1400, copying the Carolingian script of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in which they found the classics.</p></aside>
    </section>

