    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1341</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> is crowned poet laureate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1397</span><span class="rf-text">The Byzantine scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras">Manuel Chrysoloras</a> begins teaching Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1416–1417</span><span class="rf-text">Poggio Bracciolini finds complete texts of Quintilian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a> in monastery libraries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1440</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> shows that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a> is a forgery</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1462</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a> the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> to translate into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1495</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> begins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> the Greek classics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1516</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> publishes the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> at Basel, with a revised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation and notes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1599</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>’ Ratio Studiorum makes the humanist course of study the rule in their colleges</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/petrarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrarch.jpg" alt="Petrarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francesco_Petrarca_(called_Petrarch)_-_French_school,_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 14th century the cities of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> were among the richest and most literate places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and dozens of smaller communes and lordships needed men who could write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for contracts, letters, laws and speeches, and the notaries, lawyers and chancery secretaries who did so formed a large lay educated class. The universities taught law and medicine, and a philosophy built on logic and on commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, known as scholasticism. Grammar and rhetoric were practical skills rather than paths to wisdom. All around, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> above all, stood the ruins and inscriptions of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, whom Italians regarded as their own ancestors.</p><p>A new enthusiasm for imitating the ancient poets and historians began among the lawyers and judges of Padua in the late 13th century. The judge Lovato Lovati studied the metres of Seneca’s tragedies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertino-mussato">Albertino Mussato</a> wrote a Latin tragedy in Seneca’s manner, the Ecerinis, on the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano</a>, for which Padua crowned him with laurel in 1315. Some historians therefore trace the beginnings of humanism to Padua rather than to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>.</p><p>It was Francesco Petrarca, Petrarch, who gave the movement its programme and its fame. Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> in 1304 and brought up near the papal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, he gave up the study of law for letters. He hunted for the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, finding the speech Pro Archia at Liège in 1333 and Cicero’s letters to Atticus at Verona in 1345, and he wrote letters of his own to Cicero, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and other ancient authors as if to friends. He saw the centuries since the fall of Rome as an age of darkness and longed for a return to its eloquence and virtue. His crowning as poet laureate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> in Rome in 1341 announced that the honours of antiquity could live again. In later works such as On His Own Ignorance and That of Many Others (1367) he attacked the followers of Aristotle in the universities as clever in logic but of no help in living well.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola.jpg" alt="Giovanni Pico della Mirandola" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cristofano dell'Altissimo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The humanists took the name of their subjects from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>: the studia humanitatis, the studies of humanity, which came to mean grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history and moral philosophy, learned from the best <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and, later, Greek authors. By the end of the 15th century Italian students called a teacher of these subjects a humanista, as they called a teacher of law a legista; the word humanism itself came into use only in the early 19th century, when the German educator Friedrich Immanuel Niethammer gave the name Humanismus, in 1808, to a schooling based on the classics. Humanists did not reject theology, law or medicine, but they held that eloquence joined to wisdom made good men and useful citizens, and that history and moral philosophy taught better than logic how to live and how to govern. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> the chancellors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coluccio-salutati">Coluccio Salutati</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> put these skills at the service of the republic, defending its liberty against the Visconti rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>; Bruni’s Panegyric to the City of Florence and his History of the Florentine People presented Florence as the heir of the Roman republic. Other humanists served princes and popes, and wrote treatises on the education of rulers for the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, the Gonzaga of Mantua and the Aragonese royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>Their method was philology. Humanists compared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, corrected the errors of copyists and studied how words and styles changed over time, so that a text could be dated and judged by its language. They sought to write Latin as Cicero had written it rather than the Latin of the medieval schools, and from the end of the 14th century many learned Greek to read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and the Greek historians in the original. “To the sources”, ad fontes, became their watchword, and northern humanists later applied it to the Bible and the early Church Fathers.</p><p>Most humanists were devout Christians, and their programme was not a secular one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oration-on-the-dignity-of-man">Oration on the Dignity of Man</a>, written in 1486 to introduce his 900 theses, celebrated the human freedom to rise or fall by choice; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a> sought to reconcile Platonic philosophy with Christian faith; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> called his own ideal the “philosophy of Christ”, a piety drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> rather than from ceremonies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo de' Medici" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cosimo_di_Medici_(Bronzino).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Bronzino, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> became the first centre of the movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coluccio-salutati">Coluccio Salutati</a>, chancellor of the Republic of Florence from 1375 to 1406, gathered younger scholars around him and in 1397 brought the Byzantine diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras">Manuel Chrysoloras</a> to teach Greek; among those who learned from him was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a>, who went on to translate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The hunt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> reached its height during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> (1414–1418), when Poggio Bracciolini, an official of the papal court, searched monastery libraries north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and found a complete text of Quintilian’s Institutes of Oratory at St Gall in 1416 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>’s lost poem On the Nature of Things in 1417. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a>, master of Florence from 1434, paid for manuscripts and copyists and placed the books of the collector Niccolò Niccoli in the library of San Marco, opened in the 1440s.</p><p>The council that met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and then Florence in 1438–1439 to reunite the Greek and Latin churches brought Byzantine scholars to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">Gemistos Plethon</a> lectured in Florence on Plato, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarion">Bessarion</a>, made a cardinal of the Roman Church in 1439, settled in Italy and later gave his great library of Greek manuscripts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. More scholars came west before and after the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> in 1453. In 1440 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a>, in the service of Alfonso V of Aragon, then at war with the pope over the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, used the history of Latin words and institutions to show that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a>, long cited in support of the popes’ worldly power, could not have been written in Constantine’s time. In 1462 Cosimo de' Medici gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a> Greek manuscripts of Plato to translate; his complete Latin Plato was printed in 1484.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> carried the movement across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The first press in Italy was set up at Subiaco near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1465, and Venice soon became the busiest printing city in Europe. There, from 1495, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> issued the first printed editions in Greek of Aristotle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Plato, and from 1501 small portable classics in a new italic type. North of the Alps, Emperor Frederick III crowned Conrad Celtis poet in 1487; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> published the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> at Basel in 1516; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegium-trilingue">College of the Three Languages</a> for Latin, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> in 1517; and in 1530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, urged by Guillaume Budé, appointed royal lecturers in Greek and Hebrew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the beginning of the Collège de France.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-library.jpg" alt="Vatican Library" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melozzo_da_Forl%C3%AC_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Melozzo da Forlì, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>’s friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a>, author of the Decameron, wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> encyclopedia of the pagan gods and lodged the Calabrian scholar Leontius Pilatus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> while he made the first Latin translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, around 1360. In the following generations the leaders were the chancellors Salutati and Bruni; Poggio Bracciolini, the finder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>; Niccolò Niccoli, who spent his fortune on books; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a>, the sharpest critic of texts of his age; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a>, who wrote on painting, architecture and the family; Ficino, the translator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>; and Pico della Mirandola. Later in the 15th century Angelo Poliziano, tutor to the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, showed how to judge manuscripts by their age and descent.</p><p>Teachers carried the programme to a wider public. Vittorino da Feltre ran a boarding school at Mantua from 1423 for the children of the Gonzaga lords and for poor pupils of talent, and Guarino da Verona, a pupil of Chrysoloras, taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> for some thirty years; their former pupils became the teachers, secretaries and diplomats of the next generation. A few women won fame as humanists in a world that barred them from universities and public office: Isotta Nogarola of Verona, whose Latin dialogue of 1451 debated whether Adam or Eve bore the greater guilt for the Fall, Cassandra Fedele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Laura Cereta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>.</p><p>Patrons made the movement possible: the Medici in Florence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a> at Urbino, Alfonso V at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, Pope Nicholas V, who commissioned Latin translations of the Greek historians and gathered the books from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> grew, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a>, himself a humanist writer. Behind them stood a wider following of notaries, schoolmasters, students, scribes and booksellers such as Vespasiano da Bisticci of Florence. In the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus of Rotterdam</a>, Thomas More in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Johannes Reuchlin, who published a grammar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> in 1506, Guillaume Budé in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Spaniard Juan Luis Vives turned the methods of the Italians to scripture, law and the reform of society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/erasmus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erasmus.jpg" alt="Erasmus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holbein-erasmus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Holbein the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1600 almost all the surviving literature of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and much of that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was in print, edited, translated and annotated. Humanists had built the tools of modern scholarship: textual criticism, the dating of documents by their language, the study of inscriptions, coins and ruins pioneered by antiquarians such as Flavio Biondo and Cyriac of Ancona, and grammars and dictionaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. The humanist school became the standard school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Johann Sturm’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at Strasbourg, founded in 1538, the Latin schools reformed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> in the Lutheran lands, the grammar schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the colleges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, whose Ratio Studiorum of 1599 laid down a course of grammar, the humanities and rhetoric for their fast-growing network of schools.</p><p>Humanist learning was also drawn into the religious conflicts of the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> into German from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>’s Greek text, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, Melanchthon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> were humanists by training, as were many Catholic reformers. But the division of Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> split the humanists themselves. Erasmus argued for free will against Luther in 1524 and remained in the Catholic Church, yet after his death his works were placed on the papal Index of Prohibited Books of 1559; Thomas More, who wrote against the reformers, was executed in 1535 for refusing to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> as head of the Church in England.</p><p>The movement had limits. Its Latin learning belonged to a small and mostly male elite, and the ideal of reviving ancient eloquence could harden into the pedantry that Erasmus mocked in his Ciceronianus of 1528. Writers turned increasingly to the vernacular languages, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> Pietro Bembo’s Prose della volgar lingua (1525) made the Tuscan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a> the model for literary Italian. By the late 16th century humanism had ceased to be a movement of innovators and had become the ordinary content of education and learning, practised by late humanists such as Michel de Montaigne, whose Essays first appeared in 1580, and Justus Lipsius.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius.jpg" alt="Aldus Manutius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_d%27Alde_Manuce,_en_buste,_de_profil_dirig%C3%A9_%C3%A0_droite_-_estampe_-_btv1b84007044_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rota, Martinus Sibenicensis (1520?-1583). Graveur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism left European culture its idea of the humanities: the belief that the study of languages, literature, history and philosophy forms judgement and character. Schooling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek on the humanist model remained the education of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s elites into the 20th century. Its methods of textual criticism, refined by later scholars, underlie modern editions of the classics and the historical study of the Bible, and its habit of dividing history into an ancient age, a middle age and a modern revival gave Europe its periods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a>’s History of the Florentine People already set a middle period between the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the revival of the Italian cities, and the Latin phrase media tempestas, “middle time”, is first recorded in 1469. Even the letters on most printed pages descend from humanist handwriting, through the roman type adopted by printers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the 1460s and the italic first cut for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a> in 1501.</p><p>Historians have judged the movement very differently. In The Civilization of the Renaissance in Italy (1860) Jacob Burckhardt made the revival of antiquity part of a wider awakening of individualism and of the “discovery of the world and of man”. Hans Baron argued in 1955 that the Florentine humanists’ struggle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> around 1400 created a “civic humanism” devoted to republican liberty, a thesis that later scholars have contested on its dating and its evidence. Paul Oskar Kristeller defined humanism more narrowly, as a cultural and educational programme based on rhetoric and classical studies that lived alongside scholastic philosophy rather than replacing it, while Eugenio Garin stressed its philosophical seriousness. Anthony Grafton and Lisa Jardine argued in 1986 that humanist schools trained obedient servants of princes as much as free minds, and Ronald Witt traced the movement’s origins to the Paduan lawyers of the 13th century.</p><p>The humanists’ own picture of a dark <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> has also been revised. Medieval monks and scholars had copied and studied many Latin classics, and much of Greek philosophy and science had reached the West through Latin translations from Arabic and Greek in the 12th and 13th centuries. The humanists’ achievement lay less in discovering antiquity than in reading it afresh, as the record of a distant world to be understood on its own terms.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and his followers in 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, set out to revive the language, literature and moral wisdom of classical antiquity. Its scholars recovered and printed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek classics, founded the methods of textual criticism and spread a new education across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, shaping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, modern scholarship and the teaching of the humanities for centuries.</em></p>

